Apple is testing a softer appearance for iPhone navigation bars that would bring back part of the look used in iOS 26, according to a new finding shared by <a href="https://x.com/itspdfu/status/2122427326835114140">pdfu on X (Twitter)</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/gradual-fade-nav-bar-flag-pdfu-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="2560" height="1280" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1077843" /><div class="center-wrap"><em>The gradual fade nav bar effect is disabled by default in iOS 27.3 beta 2</em></div><br/>
The change affects how content fades out as it scrolls beneath the bar at the top of an app, so that text and other elements disappear into a gentler blur, rather than meeting a more sharply defined boundary.<br/>
<br/>
In the side-by-side comparison above, shared by pdfu, the difference is shown with the test feature switched off – the navigation bar has a distinct lower edge separating it from the list below. With the feature enabled (as shown on the right), the boundary is replaced by a softer, more gradual transition.<br/>
<br/>
According to pdfu, the effect is designed to appear when Liquid Glass tint is set to 25 percent or lower, and it only applies to navigation bars that use the default style. The gentler fade is currently disabled behind an internal feature flag in the latest iOS 27.3 developer beta, so it's not at a stage where users can simply turn on the change via Settings.<br/>
<br/>
Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/10/how-liquid-glass-is-changing-in-ios-27/">reworked Liquid Glass in iOS 27</a> with changes aimed at improving readability, including a more uniform background behind content at the top of apps. It also replaced the earlier Clear and Tinted presets with a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/how-to/ios-27-tone-down-liquid-glass-transparency/">continuous transparency slider</a>, offering users finer control over the Liquid Glass appearance.<br/>
<br/>
